---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import inventory from "../../figures.json";

const page = inventory.figures.filter((figure) => figure.page === "usgs");
const group = (name: string) => page.filter((figure) => figure.group === name);
---

<Base
  title="USGS earthquakes — ofantini.com"
  description="The problem a public earthquake feed poses, then the path the scheduled job runs, then the numbers the architecture and the roadmap record."
  canonical="https://ofantini.com/showcase/usgs"
  current="usgs"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Showcase</p>
      <h1 transition:name="title">USGS earthquakes</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The problem first. Then the path the scheduled job runs: dlt, then DuckLake on Cloudflare
        R2, then dbt, then Dagster. Then the numbers, each one copied from the architecture or the
        roadmap. The decision to adopt that path as the warehouse is still open.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-problem enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The problem</p>
      <h2>A small public feed, and a stack that is usually much larger.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The United States Geological Survey publishes recent earthquakes as a public feed. It is
          the right first source because there is nothing to authenticate, the shape is stable, and
          the volume is small. The work is the conveyor, not the feed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The usual answer to a famous dataset is a platform built for data far bigger than this.
          The problem here is the opposite: keep a copy that can be re-run and reconciled, on the
          one small node, and publish the numbers the runs actually produced.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("problem").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-solution enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The solution</p>
      <h2>What the scheduled job runs.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This is the path named by the orchestration section and by the roadmap item that is
          already done. It is not, by itself, the acceptance of the warehouse decision. That
          distinction is in the open section below.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step">
          <h3>dlt</h3>
          <p>
            A library, not a server. The run starts, pages the feed, keeps a cursor, writes, and
            exits. Between runs it holds no memory. The asset asks for the DuckLake destination.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>DuckLake on R2</h3>
          <p>
            DuckDB is the engine. DuckLake is the table format: files plus a catalog, so a load can
            merge. Postgres holds the catalog. The files are the R2 bucket the lake target names.
            The node's disk does not keep the history.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>dbt</h3>
          <p>
            SQL models, run as a job, in layers. Bronze is what the load wrote. Silver is
            <code>stg_usgs__events</code>: the latest revision of each event, typed, times in UTC.
            Gold is <code>fct_earthquakes</code>, earthquakes only, with magnitude and depth
            classes, and <code>agg_earthquakes_daily_region</code>. The build runs the tests with
            the models, including reconciliation of row counts across the layers.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Dagster</h3>
          <p>
            The scheduler. Rows do not pass through it. It starts the load, then the build, and it
            releases the memory when the run ends. A second job merges small files and expires old
            snapshots. One instance per environment, so staging does not touch prod.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("solution").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Measured</p>
      <h2>Rows, time and memory on the lake path.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          These are the figures the roadmap and the architecture record for the runs after Dagster
          took the job.           A sample is not a peak when the document says the samples miss short
          peaks. The CI row is a fixture, not a production load.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("lake").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-problem enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Still open</p>
      <h2>What this page does not settle.</h2>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("open").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-quiet enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Earlier path</p>
      <h2>While the layers lived in Postgres.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Before the scheduled job above, a CronJob ran the load and the build in Postgres. Those
          measurements are kept here so the later numbers are not asked to stand for them. The
          retirement date is on the staging card.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("postgres").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band enter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Rebuild</p>
      <h2>The re-load during the move.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The move to the current node re-loaded this feed. The runbook records the two runs below
          and does not name which store they wrote, so this page does not name it either.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figures">
        {
          group("drill").map((item) => (
            <Figure
              id={item.id}
              display={item.display}
              value={item.value}
              label={item.label}
              detail={item.detail}
              source={item.source}
              href={item.href}
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
